   "subject": "Re: [r-inla] Model crash when applying type-III spatiotemporal interaction term",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "You can try to reduce the number of threads, which will reduce the\r<br>amount of memory (option num.threads)\r<br>\r<br>also control.inla=list(strategy=&quot;adaptive&quot;)\r<br>\r<br>would help. \r<br>\r<br>It looks like you value for h=0.77 is very high, and the default value\r<br>as of now, is 0.005. \r<br>\r<br>You may also want to try the new testing version of today which is\r<br>faster (then remove restart, optimiser), and you may want to check out\r<br>the PARDISO library, see \r<br>\r<br>inla.pardiso()\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "\r<br>\r<br>\r<br>\r<br>On Tue, 2020-08-18 at 17:23 -0700, Insang Song wrote:\r<br>&gt; Hello,\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; I am currently working on a regression model including a random slope\r<br>&gt; with a spatiotemporal interaction term. Since I wanted to compare\r<br>&gt; results from four different types of spatiotemporal interaction, I\r<br>&gt; tried\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; form.m42c &lt;- y ~ \r<br>&gt;   f(GEOID, model = &quot;bym&quot;, graph = graph, constr = TRUE, hyper =\r<br>&gt; hyp.prior) +\r<br>&gt;   f(year1, model = &#39;ar1&#39;, constr = TRUE) + x2 + x3 + x4 + x5 + x6 + x7\r<br>&gt; + x8\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; form.t42III &lt;- update(form.m42c, ~ . + f(year3, x1, model = &quot;iid&quot;,\r<br>&gt; group = GEOID2, \r<br>&gt;                                          control.group = list(graph =\r<br>&gt; graph, model = &#39;besag&#39;),\r<br>&gt;                                       constr = TRUE) )\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; finla &lt;- inla(formula = form.t42III, data = data.pu, \r<br>&gt;               control.compute = list(waic = TRUE, dic = TRUE, cpo =\r<br>&gt; TRUE,\r<br>&gt;                                        openmp.strategy = &#39;large&#39;), \r<br>&gt;               verbose = TRUE,\r<br>&gt;               control.inla = control.inla(cmin = -0.0, h=0.77,\r<br>&gt; b.strategy = &#39;keep&#39;, strategy = &quot;simplified.laplace&quot;, \r<br>&gt;                                             int.strategy = &quot;eb&quot;,\r<br>&gt; restart = 3, optimiser = &#39;gsl&#39;,\r<br>&gt;                                             stupid.search.factor =\r<br>&gt; 1.25), #diagonal = 1e4\r<br>&gt;               control.fixed = list(prec.intercept = 0.0001),\r<br>&gt;               control.predictor = list(compute = TRUE),\r<br>&gt;               control.mode = list(restart = FALSE, fixed = FALSE))\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; However, the model consumed all of my memory then crashed. I think my\r<br>&gt; machine has a decent amount of RAM (64GB, FYI), and there was no\r<br>&gt; problem with fitting models with type-I, II, and IV spatiotemporal\r<br>&gt; interaction term.\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Could you explain why the model with the type-III spatiotemporal\r<br>&gt; interaction term is only crashed?\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Thank you very much.\r<br>&gt; \r<br>"
